{
  "dom_id": "step-q348321",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069768/assets/e51b807bc238b140215a8a753fcfe778b14735e723dd5cb43b8aab22039a8e8e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0,$ the particle is located at the position $x=-2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=4?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=-2$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=4.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(4)=-2+12=10.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=10.$",
